            <ArticleTitle>Entrainment of vastus medialis complex activity differs between genders.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>633-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/mus.24897</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">That the vastus medialis oblique (VMO) is a functional unit of the vastus medialis (VM) is disputed. Delayed VMO activation predicts patellofemoral pain, which has higher rates in women.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Single MUs and surface electromyogram (EMG) were collected from the VMO and VM of 9 men and 9 women. Men were tested once; women were tested during 5 menstrual phases. Coherence was assessed for motor unit (MU) firings within and between the VM and VMO using multilevel logistic models to determine statistical significance.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Compared with women, men have 741% (MU pairs) and 256% (MU-EMG pairs) greater odds of common drive (0-5 Hz) coherent oscillations. MU pairs from the VMO and the dual VM/VMO complex have 228% and 212% greater odds of coherent oscillations in the beta band (15-35 Hz) compared with VM pairs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The VM and VMO are neurologically different muscles; control of the VM complex is sexually dimorphic.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2015 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
